The  command  enables  system wide tracing of kernel lock events,
executes the specified command, and when finished reports statis‐
tics to the user.  Tracing may be ended early by sending (Ctrl‐C)
to the process being executed  by  lockstat.   The  pseudo‐device
driver must be present in the kernel, and the command may only be
used  by  the  root user.  The options are as follows: Adjust the
number of trace buffers allocated by the kernel  to  Report  per‐
centage  of  total events by count, and sort the output by number
of events.  The default is to  key  on  event  timings.   Disable
lockstat.   This  is  useful  when  a program crashed leaving the
lockstat kernel driver enabled.  Limit tracing  to  one  type  of
event.   Use  the  option to list valid events.  List valid event
types for the option and exit.  Limit tracing to  locking  opera‐
tions  performed within the specified function.  must be the name
